  "text": "Solution 1 is the most plausible of the three, but I could not verify it.\n\n- **Solution 2** answers 1. Its argument that size 1 is impossible is unsound: the one-element universe {0} gives a one-element quotient, and the claim that every nontrivial universe must contain * does not rule out small quotients. Its later argument that odd sizes ≥ 3 are realizable is also a sketch, not a construction.\n- **Solution 3** answers 0 by claiming every positive integer is realizable. That is asserted without a construction, and the problem's wording (\"sum of positive integers that cannot be realized\") suggests a nonempty exceptional set.\n- **Solution 1** answers 8 (3 + 5). Its impossibility arguments are hand-wavy (\"verified explicitly for all candidate heap games\" is not shown), and its size-2 and size-4 examples are asserted rather than checked. I recall that the partizan misère-quotient literature (Siegel and coauthors) identifies a small finite set of exceptional orders, and 3 and 5 is my best recollection of that set. That recollection is not something I can confirm here, so this choice rests on partial recall rather than verified work.\n\nCHOICE: 1",
